1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:15,936
Medication follows a justified clinical problem, not a single screen
value. Low saturation does not automatically mean naloxone, nausea
does not explain its own cause, and low blood pressure does not
automatically mean tranexamic acid.

2
00:00:15,936 --> 00:00:29,880
First support the immediate threat, then check whether the drug fits
the history and examination. Verify contraindications and the actual
license, endorsement, service authorization and supervision that
apply.

3
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:40,200
A school module can teach a drug before the weekend where hands-on
administration is assessed. Knowledge here is not independent
permission for a student to administer it.

4
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:52,776
All calculation cases here are original simulation exercises, not
prescriptions or school examination answers. Practise the reasoning,
preparation check and response assessment together.

5
00:00:52,776 --> 00:01:04,536
Naloxone competitively blocks opioid effects. It is used for suspected
opioid-related respiratory depression, not as a universal treatment
for every unresponsive person.

6
00:01:04,536 --> 00:01:16,968
A patient with poor breathing needs airway and ventilation support
immediately while naloxone is prepared. Waiting for a drug response
while ventilation remains inadequate is not airway-first care.

7
00:01:16,968 --> 00:01:29,856
Assess spontaneous respiratory effort, depth, air movement, mentation
and relevant monitoring after each action. The goal is effective
breathing, not necessarily full alertness.

8
00:01:29,856 --> 00:01:43,032
Pinpoint pupils may support suspicion but do not establish the
diagnosis, and their absence does not exclude opioid involvement.
Consider glucose, trauma, stroke and co-exposures.

9
00:01:43,032 --> 00:01:55,896
The current PCP adult schedule gives point four milligrams, then point
four, then point eight, then two, by the listed intramuscular or
intravenous routes at three-minute intervals as required.

10
00:01:55,896 --> 00:02:09,072
The fourth dose prefers the intravenous route, and further doses
require CliniCall consultation first. The source phrase point four
repeated four times is not an accurate description of the escalating
schedule.

11
00:02:09,072 --> 00:02:20,568
Check concentration every time. A point four milligram dose is one
millilitre at point four milligrams per millilitre, but point four
millilitres at one milligram per millilitre.

12
00:02:20,568 --> 00:02:38,928
The public pediatric section lists weight-based IM dosing at point one
milligram per kilogram, maximum two milligrams per dose, repeated
every three minutes up to four doses under its labeled license
section. Do not infer a pediatric IV authorization from the adult
line.

13
00:02:38,928 --> 00:02:53,136
Abrupt reversal can cause withdrawal, agitation, vomiting and
cardiovascular changes. Prepare to protect the airway, maintain scene
safety and reassess rather than assuming awakening completes care.

14
00:02:53,136 --> 00:03:07,656
The monograph gives an onset around one to three minutes and a
duration around thirty to forty-five minutes for its IM or IV use.
Many opioids outlast it, so recurrent respiratory depression remains
possible.

15
00:03:07,656 --> 00:03:23,952
The listed contraindications include hypersensitivity and neonates.
Palliative-care reversal is separately cautious, with the source
describing point one milligram IM or IV per minute titration to
breathing while preserving analgesia.

16
00:03:23,952 --> 00:03:36,144
Use the appropriate clinical plan and consultation for the individual
situation. A printed duration is not a safe discharge timer, and a
temporary response does not exclude another cause.

17
00:03:36,144 --> 00:03:50,208
Acetylsalicylic acid reduces platelet aggregation through irreversible
cyclooxygenase inhibition. It helps limit further thrombotic activity;
it is not a clot-dissolving drug or a treatment for every chest
symptom.

18
00:03:50,208 --> 00:04:01,464
The adult monograph lists one hundred and sixty-two milligrams orally,
chewed and swallowed. Two eighty-one-milligram tablets provide that
amount when the product is appropriate.

19
00:04:01,464 --> 00:04:14,424
Verify the ischemic indication and what has already been taken for
this event. Check the current ACS pathway rather than giving an
unverified extra dose solely because the ambulance has arrived.

20
00:04:14,424 --> 00:04:27,504
Confirm that oral administration is safe and document time, dose and
response. Continue cardiac assessment and the transport pathway;
giving ASA does not complete ACS care.

21
00:04:27,504 --> 00:04:38,688
Ask about the actual reaction to ASA or related drugs. A history of
drug-triggered bronchospasm is more specific than simply noting that
the patient has asthma.

22
00:04:38,688 --> 00:04:54,912
The monograph excludes active or recent bleeding, including relevant
head injury and peptic ulcer disease. Clarify the history and seek
clinical guidance when the significance is uncertain rather than
making an unsupported yes-or-no assumption.

23
00:04:54,912 --> 00:05:08,544
Pediatric administration requires prior CliniCall consultation and a
rare appropriate indication; a viral illness raises the listed Reye
syndrome concern. Consultation is not itself an indication.

24
00:05:08,544 --> 00:05:21,912
Record other antiplatelet or anticoagulant exposure and communicate
risk. Do not claim that a drug's platelet effect ending with platelet
turnover is the same as its shorter symptomatic effects.

25
00:05:21,912 --> 00:05:36,528
Dimenhydrinate reduces nausea through vestibular and central
antihistaminic and anticholinergic effects. Continue looking for the
cause of vomiting, especially if severe pain, neurological change or
shock is present.

26
00:05:36,528 --> 00:05:51,000
The current monograph distinguishes first-line motion, vertigo,
pregnancy or ondansetron-contraindication contexts from second-line
long-transport nausea after ondansetron has been ineffective for
fifteen to thirty minutes.

27
00:05:51,000 --> 00:06:07,488
The adult dose is twenty-five to fifty milligrams IV or IM every four
to six hours as needed. For someone over sixty-five or frail, the
listed dose is twelve point five milligrams because higher doses
increase delirium risk.

28
00:06:07,488 --> 00:06:19,272
The public pediatric dosing section is labeled ACP. Do not convert the
school's under-twelve restriction into an assumption of unrestricted
PCP dosing for every older child.

29
00:06:19,272 --> 00:06:33,552
For the listed fifty-milligram-per-millilitre ampoule, one millilitre
plus nine millilitres saline gives a final ten millilitres at five
milligrams per millilitre. Distinguish added diluent from final total
volume.

30
00:06:33,552 --> 00:06:50,280
In this simulation, twelve point five milligrams corresponds to two
point five millilitres of that prepared concentration. The monograph
IV rate is twenty-five milligrams per minute; this is a rate limit,
not permission for a rapid push.

31
00:06:50,280 --> 00:07:04,152
Check sensitivity to the listed drug families and use caution with
elevated ocular pressure or prostatic hypertrophy. Sedation,
dizziness, dry mouth and urinary retention are important adverse
effects.

32
00:07:04,152 --> 00:07:17,088
Alcohol, benzodiazepines and other depressants can increase sedation.
Reduced nausea with worsening alertness is not an uncomplicated
success; repeat the full relevant assessment.

33
00:07:17,088 --> 00:07:28,680
Tranexamic acid limits fibrin breakdown, helping preserve clots. It
does not seal a severed artery, supply blood volume or replace timely
definitive hemorrhage control.

34
00:07:28,680 --> 00:07:41,976
The PCP trauma indication combines shock or hypoperfusion with an
injury suggesting concealed or ongoing bleeding. A low pressure
without that injury context is not enough to establish the indication.

35
00:07:41,976 --> 00:07:55,104
Continue immediate hemorrhage control, reassessment, appropriate
resuscitation and urgent transport. Preparing a drug must not distract
the team from an uncontrolled bleed or unsafe delay.

36
00:07:55,104 --> 00:08:05,520
Maternal vaginal bleeding after twenty weeks or postpartum hemorrhage
with shock or hypoperfusion is listed separately and requires prior
CliniCall consultation.

37
00:08:05,520 --> 00:08:16,800
Check injury time against the actual planned administration time. More
than three hours, hypersensitivity and non-traumatic gastrointestinal
hemorrhage are listed exclusions.

38
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:31,584
The current PCP monograph excludes age below twelve. Its adult regimen
is two grams intravenously over one minute. Younger-child dosing
appears in the ACP section and is not a new PCP permission.

39
00:08:31,584 --> 00:08:49,704
School 7D contrasts the legacy one gram over ten minutes, followed by
one gram over eight hours, with the revised two grams over one minute.
These are different named regimens, not interchangeable rates. Do not
combine them or administer both to be safe.

40
00:08:49,704 --> 00:09:02,784
For an examination, identify which named source and version the
question specifies and clarify unresolved differences with the
instructor. Documentation does not make a wrong regimen correct.

41
00:09:02,784 --> 00:09:16,368
At one hundred milligrams per millilitre, two grams is two thousand
milligrams and requires twenty millilitres. Check the actual vial
concentration and number of containers rather than relying on their
appearance.

42
00:09:16,368 --> 00:09:28,200
The listed route is intravenous, not intramuscular or subcutaneous.
The one-minute administration period matters: too-rapid administration
can worsen hypotension.

43
00:09:28,200 --> 00:09:42,840
Verify line patency and continue monitoring perfusion and bleeding.
Record injury time, indication, eligibility checks, dose, route, start
and completion times, response and any consultation.

44
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:53,712
This is a preparation calculation for the cited current BCEHS regimen,
not a universal manufacturer instruction. Use the governing authorized
plan for the actual setting.

45
00:09:53,712 --> 00:10:05,088
Write the unit beside every quantity. One gram is one thousand
milligrams and one milligram is one thousand micrograms; confusing
these creates a thousand-fold error.

46
00:10:05,088 --> 00:10:15,072
To obtain volume, divide the required mass by concentration after
making the mass units agree. The mass units cancel and millilitres
remain.

47
00:10:15,072 --> 00:10:25,464
Then consider time separately. Knowing that twenty millilitres is the
correct dose volume does not tell you whether it should run in one
minute, ten minutes or hours.

48
00:10:25,464 --> 00:10:38,448
Check plausibility and obtain the required independent verification
before a simulated administration. Correct arithmetic cannot repair a
wrong indication, drug, route or source version.

49
00:10:38,448 --> 00:10:49,800
Drop factor is how many drops the set delivers per millilitre under
its stated conditions. It is a property of the actual tubing, not a
number chosen to simplify arithmetic.

50
00:10:49,800 --> 00:11:02,016
Macrodrip sets commonly use ten, fifteen or twenty drops per
millilitre; microdrip commonly uses sixty. Read the label and verify
the selected set before calculating.

51
00:11:02,016 --> 00:11:15,984
A microdrip set creates smaller drops but does not automatically make
every pediatric or high-risk infusion safe. Required precision, drug,
patient and local procedure determine suitable equipment.

52
00:11:15,984 --> 00:11:25,704
Gravity flow changes with height, position, resistance and movement. A
calculated clamp setting requires repeated actual delivery checks.

53
00:11:25,704 --> 00:11:36,312
Multiply the volume in millilitres by the set factor in drops per
millilitre, then divide by time in minutes. Millilitres cancel,
leaving drops per minute.

54
00:11:36,312 --> 00:11:46,392
Convert hours to minutes before using that formula. Entering one point
five hours as one point five minutes creates a sixty-fold flow error.

55
00:11:46,392 --> 00:11:56,424
Retain precision during intermediate calculations and round the final
result to a whole drop. You cannot reliably set a fraction of a
gravity drop.

56
00:11:56,424 --> 00:12:07,416
Finally estimate whether the result makes sense for the prescribed
volume and time. If it seems extreme, check units, factor and order
before any setup.

57
00:12:07,416 --> 00:12:18,864
For this original simulation, four hundred millilitres is prescribed
over ninety minutes using a fifteen-drop-per-millilitre set. All
required units are already compatible.

58
00:12:18,864 --> 00:12:28,944
Four hundred multiplied by fifteen, divided by ninety, gives sixty-six
point six seven drops per minute. Round the final value to sixty-
seven.

59
00:12:28,944 --> 00:12:40,920
A reverse check gives approximately four point four four millilitres
each minute, or two hundred and sixty-seven each hour. That is
consistent with four hundred over one and a half hours.

60
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:53,016
Set the actual drip chamber count, verify delivery and monitor the
patient and site. This example teaches rate calculation, not whether
four hundred millilitres is clinically appropriate.

61
00:12:53,016 --> 00:13:03,288
For ninety millilitres over forty-five minutes with a sixty-drop set,
ninety times sixty divided by forty-five gives one hundred and twenty
drops per minute.

62
00:13:03,288 --> 00:13:16,200
The equivalent volume rate is two millilitres per minute or one
hundred and twenty millilitres per hour. With a sixty-drop set only,
the numeric drops-per-minute and millilitres-per-hour values match.

63
00:13:16,200 --> 00:13:24,216
Do not apply that shortcut to a fifteen-drop set. Its drop count would
be one quarter as large for the same volume flow.

64
00:13:24,216 --> 00:13:33,552
A large count can be hard to control manually. The correct calculation
does not remove the need for suitable delivery equipment and close
observation.

65
00:13:33,552 --> 00:13:42,408
If the volume rate is already millilitres per hour, multiply by the
tubing factor and divide by sixty to convert hours to minutes.

66
00:13:42,408 --> 00:13:54,312
For one hundred and twenty millilitres per hour with a fifteen-drop
set, the result is thirty drops per minute. Check by dividing thirty
by fifteen: two millilitres per minute.

67
00:13:54,312 --> 00:14:04,272
The pump setting for that same volume flow would be one hundred and
twenty millilitres per hour, not thirty. Always read the displayed
unit before entering a number.

68
00:14:04,272 --> 00:14:17,040
If a medication rate is given as mass per minute, first convert
through the actual prepared concentration to volume per minute. Never
substitute mass directly into a volume-flow formula.

69
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:27,480
Suppose you observe forty drops per minute through a set labeled
twenty drops per millilitre. Divide forty by twenty to obtain two
millilitres per minute.

70
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:40,248
At an unchanged rate for twenty-five minutes, the calculated volume is
fifty millilitres. Verify with the actual fluid container and clinical
record; gravity rates are not perfectly constant.

71
00:14:40,248 --> 00:14:52,176
If you mistakenly used a ten-drop factor, you would estimate four
millilitres per minute and one hundred millilitres delivered. A wrong
factor can corrupt both setup and documentation.

72
00:14:52,176 --> 00:15:01,872
Use reverse checks to investigate inconsistencies. Do not edit the
record to match an expected volume when the actual delivery remains
uncertain.

73
00:15:01,872 --> 00:15:15,192
Gravity flow can change when the patient moves, the bag height
changes, the line bends or the ambulance starts moving. Recount and
inspect rather than assuming the first clamp position remains correct.

74
00:15:15,192 --> 00:15:28,296
Check for pain, swelling, leakage, occlusion and a disconnected line.
If a complication is suspected, stop or modify delivery according to
the appropriate procedure and seek help.

75
00:15:28,296 --> 00:15:41,184
Assess whether the therapy is helping and whether it is causing harm,
including worsening breathing or fluid-overload signs when relevant.
The correct rate for a previous plan may be wrong after the patient
changes.

76
00:15:41,184 --> 00:15:53,400
Document the actual volume, rate, changes and response. Include what
remains uncertain rather than converting a theoretical calculation
into a falsely exact delivered amount.

77
00:15:53,400 --> 00:16:05,760
Read the complete order, product and units before calculating. Use a
leading zero for a value below one, and avoid an unnecessary trailing
zero that could be read as a larger dose.

78
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:17,328
Write a dimensional calculation and perform an approximate
plausibility check. A tiny ampoule cannot reasonably provide a huge
calculated volume without prompting a recheck.

79
00:16:17,328 --> 00:16:28,776
Have the required second checker independently assess the indication,
product, calculation and route. Simply repeating your answer back is
not an independent verification.

80
00:16:28,776 --> 00:16:40,320
If the order, source or label is unclear, pause the nonessential drug
step and clarify while maintaining needed patient support. Do not
guess to keep a station moving.

81
00:16:40,320 --> 00:16:53,688
This original scenario tests monitoring after medication. Less nausea
is only one outcome; worsening alertness may indicate an adverse
effect, an error or progression of the underlying illness.

82
00:16:53,688 --> 00:17:05,976
Return to immediate clinical priorities and verify breathing,
circulation and glucose as appropriate. In parallel, review what was
actually administered, not just the planned dose.

83
00:17:05,976 --> 00:17:17,712
Inspect the preparation and record, and ask about alcohol,
benzodiazepines or other depressants. Do not anchor on the medication
if another dangerous cause fits the findings.

84
00:17:17,712 --> 00:17:29,376
Escalate and communicate the before-and-after observations, timing,
drug details and interventions. The receiving clinician needs both the
apparent benefit and the new risk.

85
00:17:29,376 --> 00:17:42,384
For each school medication, explain why it would or would not fit an
original patient example. State the mechanism in a sentence and
identify the exclusion that matters most in that case.

86
00:17:42,384 --> 00:17:54,912
Then calculate from an actual labeled simulated product. Show units,
concentration, volume and time, and have the required independent
check before a supervised practice action.

87
00:17:54,912 --> 00:18:07,008
After the instructor introduces a response or complication, reassess
and explain your next action. A correct initial calculation does not
guarantee a safe completed intervention.

88
00:18:07,008 --> 00:18:21,696
This completes the substantive monitoring, medication and infusion
teaching domains in school 7D Part A. The separate school readings and
practical verification remain additional requirements, not
automatically passed by listening.
